      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2014 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": "As the first line of defense, innate immunity plays an important role in protecting the host against pathogens. Innate lymphoid cells (ILCs) are emerging as important effector cells in the innate immune system and the cell type that regulate immune and tissue homeostases. Group 2 ILCs (ILC2s) are a subset of ILCs and are characterized by their capacity to produce large quantities of type 2 cytokines and certain tissue growth factors. In animal models, lung ILC2s are involved in allergic airway inflammation induced by exposure to allergens even in the absence of CD4(+) T cells and are likely responsible for tissue repair and recovery after respiratory virus infection. ILC2s are also identified in various organs in humans, and the numbers are increased in mucosal tissues from patients with allergic disorders. Further investigations of this novel cell type will provide major conceptual advances in our understanding of the mechanisms of asthma and allergic diseases."
      },
